      "id": 1,
      "text": "Huntington Beach was named after railroad magnate Henry Huntington, who orchestrated its development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.huntingtonbeachca.gov/government/about_hb/index.php",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city was originally called Pacific City before changing its name in 1903 to honor Henry Huntington.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huntington_Beach%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before European settlement, the Tongva people—also known as Gabrielinos—occupied the area that is now Huntington Beach.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.huntingtonbeachca.gov/government/about_hb/index.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Beach Boulevard began as a cattle route for the Rancho industry before becoming Huntington Beach's main thoroughfare.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
